---

<MarketingPage
  title="Open source Devin alternative: a guide to Shiba"
  description="How to evaluate an account-owned, approval-gated AI software engineer — how to run one bounded task before you switch a workflow."
  path="/open-source-devin-alternative/"
  type="article"
  publishedTime="2026-09-26T00:00:00Z"
  modifiedTime="2026-09-26T00:00:00Z"
  schema={articleSchema}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Open source Devin alternative', path: '/open-source-devin-alternative/' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="shell hero">
    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1.5 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] text-[10px] font-mono uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-[var(--navy)] mb-6 shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
      <span class="w-2 h-2 bg-[var(--navy)] animate-pulse"></span>
      <span>EVALUATION GUIDE · SEPTEMBER 2026</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="manifesto-title text-[var(--ink)]">
      Looking for an <span class="text-[var(--navy)]">open source Devin alternative</span>?
    </h1>
    <p class="lead text-[var(--ink-2)]">
      Most people searching for that phrase are not looking for one specific product. They are looking for one of five
      things: source you can inspect, a choice of coding agent, execution that happens off your laptop, isolation between
      tasks, or a way to review changes before they merge. This guide is about how to evaluate <strong>Shiba</strong>
      against the requirements you actually have.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="button" href="https://github.com/princepal9120/shiba" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        <span>Inspect the source</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <a class="button button-outline" href="/docs/overview/">
        <span>Read the docs</span>
      </a>
      <a class="button button-outline" href="/docs/approval-gates/">
        <span>How the approval gate works</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="shell">
    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="bias-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">00 / Read this first</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="bias-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">This guide is written by the people building Shiba</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          That is an obvious bias, so we will not dress it up. We are not claiming feature parity with Devin or any other
          product, and this is not a benchmark. We have not run a controlled comparison against Devin, so we make no
          claims about relative success rates, speed, or cost.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          The goal is narrower and more useful: help you decide whether Shiba fits the repository work you actually want
          to delegate. The repository is published under AGPL-3.0-only, so you can read the implementation, record the
          revision you evaluated, and connect any reproducible problem to a specific commit. That is the main practical
          advantage of an inspectable implementation: when something behaves oddly, you can find out why instead of filing
          a ticket.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          One boundary worth stating early. An open-source workspace is not a promise of free execution. It does not hand
          you model access, it does not make compute free, and it does not turn a managed service into a turnkey
          self-hosted one. We will come back to what that costs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="priorities-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">01 / Start here</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="priorities-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">What that phrase usually means</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Those five requirements overlap, and they are not interchangeable. A tool can match your preferred interface
          and still be wrong for your access model. Write down which one matters most before you compare anything.
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8 font-mono text-xs">
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Inspect the implementation</h3>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Look at the repository and its license first. Is the component you would actually depend on published?</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Pick your own coding agent</h3>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Check the agent catalog and the credential model. Can the agent you want authenticate in your environment?</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Run it off your laptop</h3>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Find out where compute runs, and whether your data ever leaves your account.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Isolate concurrent tasks</h3>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Look at orchestrator identity and the run registry. Can two tasks collide?</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--navy)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)] md:col-span-2">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-[var(--navy)] mb-2">Review before merge</h3>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Check the diff surface and the approval gate. Most importantly: can you refuse a run and start nothing at all?</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          A missing requirement is a reason to investigate, not a reason to assume a workaround exists. If your test
          suite depends on GPU hardware, a passing remote unit test does not replace running the application on that
          hardware.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="agents-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">02 / Agent choice</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="agents-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Choose the agent, not just the workspace</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Shiba ships an agent catalog. The sandbox image pins each agent CLI, and the orchestrator delegates to whichever
          one you select, so switching agents is a configuration change rather than a migration.
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6 font-mono text-xs">
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Available harnesses</h4>
            <ul class="text-[var(--ink-2)] space-y-1.5 list-disc pl-4">
              <li><code>opencode</code> — the default open coding agent</li>
              <li><code>claude-code</code> — Anthropic CLI, default for the Slack and Telegram lanes</li>
              <li><code>codex</code> — OpenAI CLI</li>
              <li><code>devin</code> — Devin CLI, when you already have access</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Credential reporting is honest</h4>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
              Gateway-backed agents authenticate through AI Gateway BYOK, which the Worker cannot introspect, so the
              dashboard says AI Gateway instead of pretending to know. The <code>devin</code> harness needs a Worker
              secret, and the Worker can observe whether it is present, never its value.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          For an evaluation, start with one agent you already know how to authenticate. Changing the agent, the
          repository, and the execution environment at the same time makes it very hard to tell which one caused the
          failure.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="runs-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">03 / Where the work runs</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="runs-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Decide where the work actually runs</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          This is the decision most open-source comparisons skip. Shiba runs the agent inside a Cloudflare Sandbox
          container on <strong>your</strong> Cloudflare account. The run clones the repository, edits files, runs commands,
          and produces a diff. Nothing executes on your laptop, and there is no per-seat subscription to a vendor's
          servers.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          The container is ephemeral per run, and the compute tier is a deploy-time choice from <code>lite</code> up to
          <code>standard-4</code>. Larger repositories can move up a tier without a code change.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          The trade-off is honest: you are responsible for the Cloudflare resources your runs consume. Set account budgets
          and alerts. Clearing history does not stop billing, and cancellation is best-effort rather than instantaneous.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="gate-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">04 / The important part</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="gate-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">A human approves first</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Most autonomous engineer tools treat the human as a notification recipient. Shiba treats the human as a gate.
          Every request, from any surface, becomes a <strong>pending approval</strong> before anything runs.
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 my-6 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)] font-mono text-xs text-[var(--ink-2)] overflow-x-auto">
          Dashboard · Slack · Telegram · POST /api/trigger<br />
          &nbsp;&nbsp;└── pending approval &nbsp;(repo, base branch, exact task text, harness)<br />
          &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;└── human approves<br />
          &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;└── Durable Object resolves it and starts a Sandbox run<br />
          &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;└── diff, exit code, optional pull request
        </div>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Four properties of that gate are worth stating precisely, because they are the difference between a gate and a
          checkbox.
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6 font-mono text-xs">
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">01 / Approved text is executed text</h4>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">The repo URL, base branch, task, and harness are frozen into the approval record. On approval, that frozen input is what runs.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">02 / Rejecting starts nothing</h4>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">A rejected approval resolves the record and provisions no container. There is no partial state to clean up.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">03 / A button is a pointer</h4>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Slack and Telegram cards carry an approval id, re-resolved server-side. Last week's card cannot start a run.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--navy)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--navy)] mb-2">04 / Seeing is not permission</h4>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">A signature proves the request came from Slack or Telegram, not who clicked. Each chat lane has an approver allowlist, and an unset list means nobody.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="creds-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">05 / The credential boundary</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="creds-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Where credentials are, and where they are not</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          This is the part to read twice. <strong>No real credential ever enters the container.</strong> Egress is
          intercepted in the Worker, where the real key is swapped in.
        </p>
        <ul class="text-[var(--ink-2)] space-y-2 list-disc pl-5 my-4">
          <li><strong>Model traffic.</strong> The container holds a dummy key and calls the provider directly. The Worker forwards it through your AI Gateway binding, which injects the stored credential.</li>
          <li><strong>Repository traffic.</strong> <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> is attached in the Worker, never inside the sandbox.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Outbound traffic is allowlisted <em>before</em> any handler runs. Anything not on the list cannot leave the
          container, including code the agent pulled from your repository. One exclusion is deliberate:
          <code>registry.npmjs.org</code> is <strong>not</strong> allowed in this version. It would enable
          <code>npm install</code> inside a run, and it is simultaneously the widest exfiltration channel available. The
          allowlist is intentionally smaller than your convenience.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          The GitHub credential is also scoped per run. <code>github.com</code> defaults to refusal; before a clone, the
          run scopes the forwarder to the one approved repository path. A request for any other repository gets a 403 with
          no <code>Authorization</code> header attached. Sibling repositories that share a prefix are refused too. A run
          that never scoped itself gets no credential at all.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="auto-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">06 / Automations</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="auto-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Automations are a gate with nobody standing at it</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Scheduled and webhook-triggered work cannot wait for a human who is awake, so it gets the same gate with
          stricter rules. Three controls apply together.
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-8 font-mono text-xs">
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Approval by default</h4>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">A schedule requests work. It does not authorize it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Unattended is opt-in and narrow</h4>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Only when opening a pull request is the run's only mutation, and only for repos on an explicit allowlist. A PR is reviewable and revertible. Nothing else is.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--navy)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--navy)] mb-2">A daily run budget</h4>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">A cron typo or a webhook loop otherwise burns tokens until somebody notices, and tokens, not compute, are the bill.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          The optional <code>run_when</code> condition asks a cheap model whether a plain-language precondition holds
          before the run starts. It fails closed: a model error, an empty answer, or anything not clearly affirmative
          means no run, and the reason is recorded rather than dropped.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="honest-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">07 / Failure modes</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="honest-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Failures are allowed to look like failures</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          An agent tool that reports cheerful success when a run actually failed is worse than no tool, because it moves
          the discovery work to review time. The rules here are blunt.
        </p>
        <ul class="text-[var(--ink-2)] space-y-2 list-disc pl-5 my-4">
          <li>A run that fails reports <code>error</code> with the real reason. It is never marked completed on unverified output.</li>
          <li>Results are parsed from a structured envelope, so a failed run cannot render as a successful one.</li>
          <li>A reclaimed run, one whose container disappeared while it was running, goes terminal with an explicit <code>outcome_unknown</code> rather than pretending it finished.</li>
          <li>Output is bounded and redacted for known secret patterns. This is not a guarantee against arbitrary secrets or binary data, so never put secrets in task text or in a repository.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="compare-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">08 / Compare fairly</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="compare-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Compare against your actual requirements</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Use this table to decide what to inspect. The last point in each card lists evidence to gather, not results we
          have measured for you.
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8 font-mono text-xs">
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Inspectable implementation</h4>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Full Worker, Durable Objects, dashboard, and deploy stack under AGPL-3.0. <em>Verify:</em> the component, revision, and setup you intend to use.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Agent choice</h4>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Four pinned agent CLIs with per-harness credential handling. <em>Verify:</em> authentication and tool availability in your environment.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Isolated tasks</h4>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Per-conversation Durable Object run registry and approval queue. <em>Verify:</em> a fresh checkout succeeds and concurrent tasks do not collide.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Human gate before execution</h4>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Pending approval on every surface; reject starts nothing. <em>Verify:</em> the frozen input that runs is the input you approved.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">No credentials in the sandbox</h4>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Egress interception, dummy model key, per-run GitHub scope. <em>Verify:</em> reachability of hosts you actually need, and the npm exclusion.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--navy)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--navy)] mb-2">Honest cost</h4>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Account-owned compute; budgets and alerts are your responsibility. <em>Verify:</em> current plan terms and usage for a representative task.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="bounded-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">09 / Do this first</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="bounded-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Run one bounded task before you change anything</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Pick a bug or a small improvement you understand well enough to review yourself. Give the agent a reproducible
          trigger, the expected result, and a boundary around the change. Include the checks that matter, and ask it to
          explain any check it could not run.
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 my-6 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--navy)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)] font-mono text-xs text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Fix the duplicate status message after reconnecting. Reproduce it by interrupting the connection during
          delivery and then reconnecting. Keep the existing status labels. Run the focused delivery tests, inspect the
          final diff, and report any verification you could not complete.
        </div>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Before approving, confirm the repository and the selected checkout. Run setup and a baseline check first. If the
          environment cannot install dependencies, fix that before evaluating anything, or you will judge the agent's work
          against an environment that was already broken.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Then compare the result against the original request, and record four things: setup effort, manual
          interventions, checks completed, and review time. A successful task should produce an understandable patch and
          enough evidence for you to decide what to do next.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="parallel-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">10 / Scale carefully</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="parallel-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Increase parallelism only when ownership is clear</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Once one task works end to end, try two independent changes. Give each a separate area of responsibility.
          Different chat conversations already map to different orchestrators, which is the natural isolation boundary, but
          a Durable Object separates run state rather than architectural disagreement. If both tasks need to change the
          same contract, settle the contract first, or sequence the dependent work.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Test the integrated result even when the individual branches passed their own checks. For a second opinion,
          have another session review with a read-only assignment, and keep the writer and reviewer roles clearly
          separate.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="recovery-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">11 / Reliability</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="recovery-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Check recovery as part of the evaluation</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Remote work has states that are easy to confuse. A message can be queued without executing. A transcript can
          stay readable while the compute behind it sleeps. A restored connection does not prove the agent process or the
          working files recovered.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Run a small disposable task to test this. After a reconnect, inspect the message outcome, the branch, and the
          current files before submitting the same request again. Keep the client version and workspace configuration with
          your notes. Reliability matters here because review depends on knowing which commands ran against which files.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="cost-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">12 / The real cost</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="cost-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Separate software, compute, and model costs</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">An open-source workspace is not zero-cost execution. Three things can each have a bill.</p>
        <ul class="text-[var(--ink-2)] space-y-2 list-disc pl-5 my-4">
          <li><strong>Compute</strong> — Workers, Durable Objects, and container time on your Cloudflare account.</li>
          <li><strong>Models</strong> — inference through your AI Gateway arrangement, or a separate agent subscription if you use one.</li>
          <li><strong>Your time</strong> — setup and review, which is usually the largest line item and the one nobody quotes.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Use one representative task to measure the whole workflow, including review time. More parallel tasks increase
          throughput, and they also increase the number of changes waiting for a person to inspect. That queue is a real
          cost with a real failure mode.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="limits-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">13 / What is not here yet</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="limits-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">What is deliberately not here yet</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Honesty about limits is more useful than a feature list. Today:</p>
        <ul class="text-[var(--ink-2)] space-y-2 list-disc pl-5 my-4">
          <li>Each installation is single-tenant and account-owned. There is no multi-tenant isolation.</li>
          <li><code>npm install</code> inside a run is not possible, by design.</li>
          <li>Cancellation is best-effort.</li>
          <li>The Worker checks for a Cloudflare Access header; that is not JWT verification. Keep the route behind Access and do not expose the origin.</li>
          <li>Sessions resume and stop/resume are not implemented. They are a real architecture change, not a toggle.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="fit-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">14 / Is it a fit?</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="fit-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">When Shiba is worth evaluating</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Evaluate Shiba when you want an AI software engineer that runs inside your own cloud account, that a human
          approves before anything executes, and whose code you can read. It fits well if you already run Cloudflare, if
          you need a per-repository credential boundary, and if you want agent choice without rebuilding the harness layer
          yourself.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Look elsewhere if your deciding requirement is a specific third-party integration, a managed hosting
          arrangement you do not want to operate, or byte-for-byte identical behavior with a different tool. An
          alternative can serve the same goal without being a drop-in replacement, and we would rather you find the right
          tool than ours.
        </p>
